  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Alternative Options for Sustainable Water Resources Management  and WASH in coastal areas</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Four primary physical effects have been identified as key vulnerabilities in the coastal areas of Bangladesh: saline water intrusion; drainage congestions; extreme events; and changes in coastal morphology. Two prime parameters will be considered in promoting mitigation measures to the current crisis: adaptive capacity and available resources, together with disaster resilient approach. 

Two CAFOD partners, Caritas Bangladesh and ADD will explore alternative options for managing water resources that will support the needs of people living with disabilities in the current EC-CAFOD funded multi-partner DRR livelihoods programme. 

Together with community members Caritas Bangladesh will re-excavate large and small ponds, re-establish large water bodies and provide household rain water harvesting systems. The establishment of a water management committee and awareness raising sessions on WASH, operation and maintenance of the system will aid in ensuring that these water resources are managed and maintained by the target communities.  

CAFOD and Caritas Bangladesh will collaborate with Caritas Switzerland in using WASA method, a chlorination process to purify the pond water.</narrative>
  </description>
